WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:01.560
(gentle music)

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.530
- France borders eight countries.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.650
Well, actually, no, it doesn't,

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.930
it borders way more than eight countries.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.560
And in fact, its longest border

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.390
isn't even this one with
Belgium or this one with Spain,

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.143
it's this one way over here with Brazil.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.830
What is going on here?

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.930
I learned this on Reddit,

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.930
and it quickly flung me
down a giant rabbit hole

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.160
of some very strange information.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.470
I then put a call out on Instagram,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.610
and asked all of you to tell
me what is going on with France

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.610
and its giant overseas
empire looking thing.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.343
I got a bunch of responses.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.630
So then I did what I always do,

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.750
which is spent the
entire day mapping this,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.100
every single island, every
single administrative boundary,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.680
every single ocean border.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:49.930
I made an entire behind the scenes,

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.490
by the way, for my patrons.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.300
Thank you, patrons, you guys rock.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.440
Yeah, it was a lot.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.430
But now I'm on the other side of it

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.032
and I have some stuff to share with you.

00:00:58.032 --> 00:01:02.100
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.130
By the way, this video is
sponsored by Storyblocks,

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.250
where I get a lot of amazing footage.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.103
More on that later.

00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:11.670
(upbeat instrumental music continues)

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:12.920
Okay, so this is France,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.230
but really this is only
one version of France.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.970
They call this version of France,

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.840
L'Hexagone, or the hexagon.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.410
It's also called La France Metropole,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.390
or the Metropolitan France.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.183
It's the mainland, it's
the motherland of France.

00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:27.590
It's the France you probably think of

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.070
when you think of France here in Europe.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.930
But there's another France,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.040
it's called France d'outre-mer,
or the Overseas France.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.403
And that one looks like this.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.050
This France accounts for almost
20% of all of France's land,

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.530
and a whopping 97% of its ocean territory,

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.340
nearly 3 million French
citizens live here.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.320
This France is peculiar.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.750
It includes far-flung tropical islands,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.080
remote archipelagos with penguins,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.080
and weird looking, uninhabited
atolls all around the world.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.530
There's lobster fishing,
and scientific research,

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.910
and nuclear weapons testing,
and military exercises.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:08.360
And the mind blowing fact here

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.960
is that because France
actually looks like this,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.253
that means that the European
Union actually looks like this.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.660
I wanna show you France's weirdest borders

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.630
and pieces of land to try to
understand why this country,

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.320
that should have decolonized
six centuries ago,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.461
has territory all around the world.

00:02:26.461 --> 00:02:30.128
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.360
All right, so let's go
over to the Netherlands.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.270
No, no, no, not that Netherlands.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.390
Let's go this way over to the Caribbean.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.810
This is a little island
that is split in half

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.843
between the Netherlands and France.

00:02:42.843 --> 00:02:44.630
It's the only place
where these two countries

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.490
share a border.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.980
The population here,
they're French citizens.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.320
A few years back, they did vote

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.650
to become semi-independent from France,

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.820
but even still they're French
citizens, this is France.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.070
Zoom out and you'll see
that France isn't alone.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.050
This whole neighborhood

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.530
is full of European colonial holdovers.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.840
France has three other
island groups in this chain.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.400
Two of which are basically the
exact same as mainland France

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.950
in their administrative status.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.660
And then you get down here
to France's longest border,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.100
which is in French Guiana,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.940
which is a proper part of France,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.140
and the European Union, and the eurozone.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.300
They use the euro here.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.440
But remember where we are,
we're in South America.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.360
This place was used as a prison colony

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.860
for France most of the time.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:26.960
A lot of people were sent here

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.610
for grueling prison sentences, many died.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.780
That's a whole other history,
we're not going into it.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.430
Okay, so this is French
Guiana in the Caribbean,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.470
key part of France.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.080
Honestly, people just
like to vacation here,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.160
because they speak French

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.813
and it looks like this.

00:03:40.813 --> 00:03:44.960
(mellow instrumental music)

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.660
Anyway, let's zoom up North
to a teeny, tiny island

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.010
that is a holdover from the old days

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.130
when France was colonizing North America.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.190
This little piece of
France is 4,000 kilometers

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.090
from France Metropole,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.870
but it is a few kilometers
off the coast of Canada.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.380
And it's right smack dab

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.160
in the middle of Canadian economic waters.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.257
France wanted to hold on to this

00:04:08.257 --> 00:04:11.660
and have it as their own little
enclave away from France.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.060
And so a few years ago,

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.810
they fought with Canada in
the international courts

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.790
to try to carve out a little
bit of economic waters

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.390
for themselves that looked like this.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.000
It turns out they ended up only getting

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.550
this little key-shaped
slice of economic waters,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.800
so that they could have
a little passage way

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.100
into their country from
international waters

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.500
to their little France enclave.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.560
And that's what it looks like today.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.330
It's a super weird situation.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.163
Here on this island,

00:04:39.163 --> 00:04:42.340
they use the euro and they speak French.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.580
Like France French, not Quebec French.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.120
It is a very French place,

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.170
even though it is right smack dab

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:49.500
in the middle of Canadian waters.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.060
Okay, so the Caribbean

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.207
and this weird set of
islands off of Canada.

00:04:53.207 --> 00:04:56.874
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.690
Now let's get to the wild ones.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.630
The ones that were
actually the hardest for me

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.700
to get my head around
as I was mapping them.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.367
(mellow instrumental music)

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.030
If you zoom all the way down
here, close to Antarctica,

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.670
all of these islands are owned by France.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.600
There are people who live on these islands

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.340
who are French citizens.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.340
We'll get to those in a second.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.330
But first look at this
weird looking block.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.920
I know it's a little bit awkward.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:30.900
Thanks Mercator.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.700
Let's look at it a little
bit differently here

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.760
from like an actual bottom up view.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.890
This slice of land is claimed by France.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.300
The Frenchmen arrived here in the 1800s

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.880
and claimed it for France,

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.510
and they've had people there ever since.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:44.610
That's a literal ice wall.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.740
Even in the 1800s, they knew
that there was an ice wall.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.860
The world is flat.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.250
Okay, so France claims this
little bit of Antarctica.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.500
Zoom up just a little bit,

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.580
and you'll get to all these French islands

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.630
that have a ton of inhabitants,

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.568
but they're just not humans.

00:05:58.568 --> 00:06:02.235
(mellow instrumental music)

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.530
There are more king
penguins on these islands

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.200
than anywhere else in the world.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.010
And honestly, looking at
photos of these islands

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.930
makes me feel like this
is the part of France

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.750
I want to visit more
than any part of France.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.100
I mean, look at this place.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.230
And the crest for this group of islands,

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.220
the official crest, is
like really cool looking.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.660
I mean, it's got lobsters,

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.550
and seals that are like yelling,

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:30.830
and king penguins.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.350
I mean, this place is just
a wild, freaking dreamland.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.500
The only actual people
who live on these islands

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.280
are French military and science personnel.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.000
So why did France hold on
to these remote islands

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.740
near Antarctica and these
ones here in the Indian Ocean?

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.990
Well, other than the fact that France is,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.720
as our friend CGP Grey notes.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.820
- [CGP Grey] The queen of not letting go.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.380
- These islands provide France

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.900
with a major presence in the Indian Ocean.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.870
Military presence, where
they can put bases,

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.650
they can run patrols,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.500
they can just have a presence

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.220
in this important geopolitical
part of the world.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.540
- [Narrator] Here are the
French military activities

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:11.690
in the region between 2016 and 2019.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.050
You can see how these islands give France

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.380
immense military presence in the region.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.770
- Okay, wait, I'm just trying to do

