00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.940
- Okay, that's actually
pretty effed up, yeah.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.740
Okay, so zoom to another really
remote part of the ocean,

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:05.210
this time the Pacific Ocean,

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.060
so the most remote ocean in the world,

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.130
and you will see a bunch

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:12.400
of teeny tiny islands that are France.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.760
They're 14,000 kilometers
from France Metropole,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.540
which is like a half a planet away.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.920
Like they are so remote.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.130
And to understand why France

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:23.110
wants to hold on to these islands,

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:25.040
just look at the water boundaries.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.940
The water boundaries of these 118 islands

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:32.340
of French Polynesia are 5
million square kilometers.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.220
For context, the entire European Union,

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.000
like the landmass, the
area of the whole EU,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.100
is 4.4 million square kilometers.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.040
So the water boundaries around
French Polynesia are huge.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:49.000
It is a lot of water
that belongs to France.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.180
This place is home to
Bora Bora and Tahiti,

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.880
and just a bunch of
really beautiful islands.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.180
So maybe I take back
my previous statement,

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.690
this is the part of France
I want to visit the most.

